+++
title: Hattusa
description: Capital of the Hittite Empire
hook: 'Around 1700 BC Hattusa burned, and King Anitta of Kussara took credit
  for it, erecting an inscribed curse on whoever should become king and
  resettle the place: "let the Stormgod of the Sky strike him!" It was rebuilt
  anyway, possibly by a son of Anitta.'
standfirst: Hattusa was the capital of the Hittite Empire in the late Bronze
  Age, during two distinct periods. Its ruins lie near modern Boğazkale,
  Turkey, within the great loop of the Kızılırmak River. German excavations
  begun in the early 20th century continue to this day, and the site was added
  to the UNESCO World Heritage list in 1986.
key-facts:
  - name: Where it is
    value: Near Boğazkale, Çorum Province, Turkey
    from: "location: Near Boğazkale, Çorum Province, Turkey"
  - name: First settled
    value: 6th millennium BC
    from: "built: 6th millennium BC"
  - name: Abandoned
    value: c. 1180 BC
    from: "abandoned: c. 1180 BC"
  - name: What is left
    value: In ruins
    from: "condition: In ruins"
  - name: On the World Heritage list since
    value: 1986 (10th Session)
    from: "inscription: 1986 (10th Session)"
  - name: Size of the protected site
    value: 268.46 ha (663.4 acres)
    from: "area: 268.46 ha (663.4 acres)"
pull-quotes:
  - text: Whoever after me becomes king resettles Hattusas, let the Stormgod of
      the Sky strike him!
    attribution: Inscribed curse erected by King Anitta from Kussara, who took
      credit for burning the city
    section: history
  - text: As Duthaliya the Great King shattered the Assuwa-Country he dedicated
      these swords to the Storm-God, his lord
    attribution: Akkadian inscription on a Mycenae bronze sword found on the
      western slope in 1991
    section: archaeology
timeline:
  - when: 6th millennium BC
    what: The earliest traces of settlement on the site, during the Chalcolithic
      period.
    from: The earliest traces of settlement on the site are from the 6th
      millennium BC during the [[ Chalcolithic ]] period.
  - when: Toward the end of the 3rd millennium BC
    what: The Hattian people establish a settlement on ground occupied even
      earlier, and call the site Hattush.
    from: Toward the end of the 3rd millennium BC the [[ Hattian people ]]
      established a settlement on locations that had been occupied even
      earlier and referred to the site as Hattush.[[ ^2 ]]
  - when: 19th and 18th centuries BC
    what: Merchants from Assyria, centered at Kanesh, establish a trading post in
      their own separate quarter of the lower city.
    from: In the 19th and 18th centuries BC, merchants from [[ Assyria ]],
      centered at [[ Kanesh ]] (Neša) (modern Kültepe) established a trading
      post there, setting up in their own separate quarter of the lower
      city.[[ ^3 ]]
  - when: around 1700 BC
    what: The city is burned and ruined. The responsible party appears to have
      been King Anitta from Kussara, who took credit for it.
    from: "A carbonized layer apparent in excavations attests to the burning and
      ruin of the city of Hattusa around 1700 BC. The responsible party
      appears to have been King [[ Anitta | anitta-king ]] from [[ Kussara ]],
      who took credit for the act and erected an inscribed curse for good
      measure:"
  - when: Around 1650 BC
    what: The Hittite king Labarna moves the capital from Neša to Hattusa and
      takes the name of Hattusili.
    from: Around 1650 BC, the Hittite king Labarna moved the capital from Neša to
      Hattusa and took the name of [[ Hattusili | hattusili-i ]], the "one/man
      from Hattusa".[[ ^7 ]][[ ^8 ]]
  - when: c. 1344–1322 BC
    what: The massive and still visible course of walls around the inner and outer
      city is erected, during the reign of Suppiluliuma I.
    from: At its peak, the city covered 1.8 km^^2^^ (440 acres) and comprised an
      inner and outer portion, both surrounded by a massive and still visible
      course of walls erected during the reign of [[ Suppiluliuma I ]] c.
      1344–1322 BC.
  - when: 1259 or 1258 BC
    what: The terms of a peace settlement between the Hittites and the Egyptians
      under Ramesses II, reached years after the Battle of Kadesh.
    from: "[[ One particularly important tablet | egyptianhittite-peace-treaty ]],
      currently on display at the [[ Istanbul Archaeology Museum ]], details
      the terms of a [[ peace settlement | egyptianhittite-peace-treaty ]]
      reached years after the [[ Battle of Kadesh ]] between the Hittites and
      the Egyptians under [[ Ramesses II ]], in 1259 or 1258 BC."
  - when: around 1200 BC
    what: The city is destroyed together with the Hittite state itself, as part of
      the Late Bronze Age collapse.
    from: The city was destroyed, together with the Hittite state itself, around
      1200 BC, as part of the [[ Late Bronze Age collapse ]].
  - when: c. 1180 BC
    what: The date the records give for the site's abandonment.
    from: "abandoned: c. 1180 BC"
  - when: 12th Century BC
    what: Agricultural communities with a material culture distinct from the
      Hittites settle in the remains of Hattusa.
    from: Agricultural communities with a [[ material culture ]] distinct from the
      Hittites settled in the remains of Hattusa as early as the 12th Century
      BC.[[ ^17 ]]
  - when: "1834"
    what: Charles Texier discovers monumental ruins near the town of Boğazköy.
    from: In 1833, the French archaeologist [[ Félix Marie Charles Texier |
      charles-texier ]] (1802–1871) was sent on an exploratory mission to
      Turkey, where in 1834 he discovered monumental ruins near the town of
      Boğazköy.[[ ^18 ]]
  - when: "1886"
    what: Georges Perrot is the first to suggest that Boğazköy was the Hittite
      capital of Hattusa.
    from: Perrot was the first to suggest, in 1886, that Boğazköy was the Hittite
      capital of Hattusa.[[ ^22 ]]
  - when: "1906"
    what: Hugo Winckler begins actual excavations; thousands of tablets are
      recovered, and the few Akkadian texts firmly identify the site as
      Hattusa.
    from: Winckler began actual excavations in 1906, focusing mainly on the royal
      fortress area. Thousands of tablets were recovered, most in the then
      unreadable Hittite language. The few Akkadian texts firmly identified
      the site as Hattusa.[[ ^26 ]]
  - when: "1986"
    what: Hattusa is added to the UNESCO World Heritage Site list.
    from: Hattusa was added to the [[ UNESCO World Heritage Site |
      list-of-world-heritage-sites-in-turkey ]] list in 1986.
  - when: "2011"
    what: The sphinx that had stayed in Germany is moved to the Boğazköy Museum,
      reuniting the pair near their original location.
    from: but the other remained in Germany where it was on display at the [[
      Pergamon Museum ]] from 1934 until it was moved to the Boğazköy Museum
      outside the Hattusa ruins in 2011, along with the Istanbul sphinx
      reuniting the pair near their original location.[[ ^47 ]]
image-plan:
  - file: Hattusa_reconstructed_wall.JPG
    role: hero
    section: lead
    caption: Reconstructed city walls. Hattusa displayed over 6 km (3.7 mi) of
      walls, with inner and outer layers around 3 m (9.8 ft) thick and 2 m (6
      ft 7 in) of space between, totalling 8 m (26 ft).[[ ^12 ]]
  - file: Hattusa_Yerkapi_rampant.JPG
    role: full
    section: history
    caption: The rampart at Hattusa. Inner and outer city alike were surrounded by
      a massive and still visible course of walls, erected during the reign of
      [[ Suppiluliuma I ]] c. 1344–1322 BC.[[ ^11 ]]
  - file: Hattusa,_capital_of_the_Hittite_Empire_04.jpg
    role: inline
    section: history
    caption: The Great Temple in the inner city, some 0.8 km^^2^^ (200 acres)
      occupied by a citadel with large administrative buildings and temples.[[
      ^11 ]]
  - file: Hattusa_reliefs1.jpg
    role: inline
    section: history
    caption: Reliefs and hieroglyphs from Chamber 2, built and decorated by [[
      Suppiluliuma II ]], the last king of the Hittites.
  - file: Yazilikaya_B_12erGruppe.jpg
    role: inline
    section: archaeology
    caption: Twelve Hittite gods of the Underworld at nearby [[ Yazılıkaya ]], a
      sanctuary of Hattusa, where [[ Georges Perrot ]] also excavated in
      1861.[[ ^21 ]]
  - file: Ḫattuša_Büyükkaya_04.jpg
    role: inline
    section: archaeology
    caption: Büyükkaya, the focus of Jürgen Seeher's excavations from 1994 to
      2005, along with non-monumental areas including economic and residential
      spaces.[[ ^34 ]]
  - file: Hattusa_magazzini_per_cereali.jpg
    role: inline
    section: archaeology
    caption: Large pottery standing at the excavation site.
  - file: Museum_Boğazkale_13.jpg
    role: inline
    section: archaeology
    caption: An artifact from the site of Hattusa, presented at Hattusa Museum.
  - file: Hattusa_Green_Stone_2019-12-08.jpg
    role: inline
    section: archaeology
    caption: The [[ Hattusa Green Stone ]], a monument believed to have religious
      origins.
  - file: Treaty_of_Kadesh.jpg
    role: full
    section: cuneiform-royal-archives
    caption: "The [[ Treaty of Kadesh | egyptianhittite-peace-treaty ]]: the terms
      of a peace settlement reached years after the [[ Battle of Kadesh ]]
      between the Hittites and the Egyptians under [[ Ramesses II ]], in 1259
      or 1258 BC.[[ ^46 ]]"
  - file: Sphinx_Gate,_Hattusa_01.jpg
    role: full
    section: sphinxes
    caption: The Sphinx Gate entrance of the city. A pair of [[ sphinxes | sphinx
      ]] found at the southern gate were taken to [[ Germany | german-empire
      ]] in 1917 and only reunited near their original location in 2011.[[ ^47
      ]]
glossary:
  - term: Chalcolithic
    gloss: The period, in the 6th millennium BC, to which the earliest traces of
      settlement on the site belong.
    from: The earliest traces of settlement on the site are from the 6th
      millennium BC during the [[ Chalcolithic ]] period.
  - term: Hattush
    gloss: What the Hattian people called the site when they settled it toward the
      end of the 3rd millennium BC.
    from: Toward the end of the 3rd millennium BC the [[ Hattian people ]]
      established a settlement on locations that had been occupied even
      earlier and referred to the site as Hattush.[[ ^2 ]]
  - term: karum
    gloss: The early 2nd millennium BC trade post at Hattusa, where forty
      mercantile documents in the Old Assyrian dialect of Akkadian were found.
    from: Forty mercantile documents written in the Old Assyrian dialect of
      Akkadian were found in the early 2nd millennium BC [[ karum |
      karum-trade-post ]].
  - term: Büyükkale
    gloss: '"Great Fortress": the high ridge where the royal residence, or
      acropolis, was built.'
    from: The royal residence, or [[ acropolis ]], was built on a high ridge now
      known as [[ Büyükkale ]] (Great Fortress).[[ ^11 ]]
  - term: Late Bronze Age collapse
    gloss: The wider episode, around 1200 BC, of which the destruction of Hattusa
      and of the Hittite state was a part.
    from: The city was destroyed, together with the Hittite state itself, around
      1200 BC, as part of the [[ Late Bronze Age collapse ]].
  - term: Bogazköy Archive
    gloss: "The cuneiform royal archives of clay tablets from the Hittite Empire
      New Kingdom period found at the site: correspondence and contracts,
      legal codes, cult procedure, oracular prophecies and literature."
    from: One of the most important discoveries at the site has been the cuneiform
      royal archives of clay tablets from the Hittite Empire New Kingdom
      period, known as the [[ Bogazköy Archive ]], consisting of official
      correspondence and contracts, as well as legal codes, procedures for
      cult ceremony, oracular prophecies and literature of the ancient Near
      East.
  - term: KBo and KUB
    gloss: "The two series in which tablets from the early excavations were
      published: //Keilschrifttexte aus Boghazkoi// (KB0) and //Keilschrift
      urkunden aus Boghazköi// (KUB)."
    from: Tablets from these excavations were published in two series
      //Keilschrifttexte aus Boghazkoi// (KB0) and //Keilschrift urkunden aus
      Boghazköi// (KUB).
  - term: bullae
    gloss: 'Sealed clay objects: 3400 of them, with clay lumps, were found in the
      "Westbau" building of the upper city, primarily associated with land
      documents.'
    from: Another significant find during the 1990-91 excavation season in the
      "Westbau" building of the upper city, was 3400 sealed bullae and clay
      lumps dating from the 2nd half of the 13th century BC. They were
      primarily associated with land documents.[[ ^42 ]]
further:
  - slug: assyria
    why: Merchants from Assyria, centered at Kanesh, set up a trading post at
      Hattusa in the 19th and 18th centuries BC, and forty mercantile
      documents in the Old Assyrian dialect of Akkadian were found in the
      trade post there.
  - slug: mesopotamia
    why: By the middle of the 2nd millennium the scribal community at Hattusa had
      grown up on Syrian, Mesopotamian and Hurrian input, writing the usual
      range of Akkadian and Sumerian language texts.
anchors:
  hook: >-
    A carbonized layer apparent in excavations attests to the burning and ruin
    of the city of Hattusa around 1700 BC. The responsible party appears to
    have been King [[ Anitta | anitta-king ]] from [[ Kussara ]], who took
    credit for the act and erected an inscribed curse for good measure:


    <blockquote
      Whoever after me becomes king resettles Hattusas, let the Stormgod of the Sky strike him![[ ^4 ]]

    Nevertheless the city was rebuilt, possibly by a son of Anitta.[[ ^5 ]][[
    ^6 ]]
  standfirst: >-
    !!Hattusa!!, also !!Hattuşa!!, !!Ḫattuša!!, !!Hattusas!!, or !!Hattusha!!,
    was the capital of the [[ Hittite Empire | hittites ]] in the late [[
    Bronze Age ]] during two distinct periods. Its ruins lie near modern [[
    Boğazkale ]], Turkey (originally Boğazköy), within the great loop of the
    [[ Kızılırmak River ]] (Hittite: //Marashantiya//; Greek: //[[ Halys |
    halys-river ]]//).


    French archeologist [[ Charles Texier ]] brought attention to the ruins
    after his visit in 1834. Over the following century, sporadic exploration
    occurred, involving different archaeologists. The [[ German Oriental
    Society | deutsche-orient-gesellschaft ]] and the [[ German Archaeological
    Institute ]] began systematic excavations in the early 20th century, which
    continue to this day.[[ ^1 ]] Hattusa was added to the [[ UNESCO World
    Heritage Site | list-of-world-heritage-sites-in-turkey ]] list in 1986.
